:: The Crack [event main_quest] {"position":"5450,500","size":"100,100"}
/* Quest "The Crack" (main_crack), STAGE1_PROPOSAL.md 4.10. A weekend. $ethan.name asked for the office to himself for some
   "stability checks", and I drop by anyway. Through the glass of the meeting room I find him sitting in front of a test rig
   with a timer running, eyes closed, and on the rig's screen a firmware build from the headset days, the one without a cap.
   Ten minutes. When it wears off he looks at me with something I can't read. I pretend not to notice anything, and he tells
   me everything is stable. The first small crack, and I ignore it. No foreshadowing beyond what I see: he never says why.
   The Monday after is the next quest, main_crackLogs (The Crack 2). */
<<silent>><<SetBackground $locations[13].img>><</silent>>
The office sounds different on a Saturday. Without the keyboards and the coffee machine and $ethan.name narrating his own code under his breath, all that's left is the vent and the fridge in the kitchen, taking turns.

He told me on Friday that he wanted the place to himself this weekend, for stability checks on the Catalyst firmware, long runs, nothing that needed anybody watching. He said it while he was packing up, casually, with his back to me, and I said sure. Then I thought about it all evening, and this morning I bought two coffees and came in anyway.

His jacket is on his chair and his laptop is open on his desk, locked. The light is on in the meeting room.

<<linkreplace "Look through the glass">>
The blinds are half down. Through the gap I can see him.

He's sitting in a chair at the end of the table, facing one of $brian.name's test rigs: a bare circuit board on a stand, one emitter, a thin cable running to a second laptop. His phone is on the table next to him with a timer running. He has his hands flat on his thighs and his eyes closed, and he's smiling, a small, slack, faraway smile I've never seen on him, the kind people have in the dentist's chair after the gas.

On the laptop screen, in a terminal window, there's a build name I haven't seen in more than a year. I know it because I named it, at two in the morning, the week the headset first talked back instead of only listening.

It's the old code, from before I decided how high the dial is allowed to go. The version without a cap.

<<Media "images/events/the_crack/glass.jpg" 600>>
/* Through half-closed blinds: Ethan in a chair facing a bare test rig, eyes closed, a faint smile, a phone timer on the table */

<<linkreplace "Wait">>
I don't go in. I stand at the glass with two coffees getting cold in my hands and watch the timer on his phone count down. 6:12. 6:11.

He doesn't move. Once, his fingers curl on his thighs and uncurl again. Once, he laughs, softly, at nothing, the way you laugh in a dream.

I think about the vent above my desk, which has been set to eighteen percent since the day we moved in, and about the late night I switched it off and he laughed at my jokes anyway. I try not to think about what it would feel like to sit where he's sitting at full strength, mostly because I already know.

The timer hits zero, the phone buzzes against the table, and the emitter on the rig goes dark.

<<linkreplace "Watch it wear off">>
It takes a minute. The smile goes first, slowly, like water running out of a sink, and then his hands close into fists on his thighs. After a while he opens his eyes and just sits there, looking at the rig and breathing.

Then he turns his head and sees me through the glass.

There's something on his face I can't read, something quieter than guilt or fear. He looks at me the way you look at a photo of someone you thought you knew, when you notice something in the background you never saw before.

Then it's gone, and he's $ethan.name again, standing up and grinning and opening the door.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $ethan, text: "Hey, boss! What are you doing here, it's Saturday. Don't you have a life? Actually, don't answer that, I've seen your weekends." },
{ who: $player, text: "I brought you coffee. It was hot when I got here." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Cold coffee is still coffee. I got through four years of high school on that rule." },
{ text: "He takes one and drinks half of it in one go. His hand is steady. He closes the laptop on the table behind him without looking at it, the way you close a book you've finished." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

<<linkreplace "Ask how it's going">>
<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "So how are the stability checks going?" },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Stable. Really, boringly stable. I've been staring at numbers since eight and my eyes are about to fall out of my head." },
{ who: $player, text: "Did you find anything?" },
{ text: "He looks at me over the coffee cup for a moment before he answers." },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Nope, nothing. Everything does exactly what it says on the box." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

I could ask him about the build name on the screen, or why the rig was pointed at his chair, or what it felt like.

I don't. If I ask, he'll answer, and then we'll both know that I know, and something between us will have to change. It's a lot easier to leave it as a Saturday and some cold coffee. I've always been pretty good at deciding which things didn't happen.

<<silently>>
<<set _msgs = [
{ who: $player, text: "Don't stay here all weekend, okay?" },
{ who: $ethan, text: "Wouldn't dream of it, just a couple more hours. You go home, boss, go outside for once. I hear they've got a sun out there now and people really like it." },
]>>
<</silently>>
<<Chat _msgs>>

He goes back into the meeting room and closes the door. Through the gap in the blinds I watch him sit down at the second laptop and start typing, fast, the way he types when he's working, and he doesn't look up again.

I leave the other coffee on his desk next to his locked laptop. On the stairs I notice that my hands aren't completely steady, which is strange, because nothing happened.

<<UpdateStat "+" "Corruption" 1>>
<<NextQuest $player>>
<<ReturnFromEvent "Go home" "Bedroom">>
<</linkreplace>>
